          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Years of Friedrich Engels</strong><br>\nFriedrich Engels was born in 1820 in Barmen, Prussia, now part of Germany. He was raised in a wealthy textile family, which provided him with insights into the workings of the industrial business and the conditions of the working class.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Introduction to Social and Political Theory</strong><br>\nEngels' early work in his family's business took him to Manchester, England, where he witnessed the poor living conditions of factory workers. This experience shaped his social and political views and led to his lifelong collaboration with Karl Marx.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Collaboration with Karl Marx</strong><br>\nEngels met Karl Marx in Paris in 1844, and they discovered a mutual intellectual and political kinship. Their partnership was pivotal, with Engels contributing to the development of Marxist theory, which sought to analyze and address the issues of capitalism and class struggle.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
