          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>\"The Condition of the Working Class in England\"</strong><br>\nIn 1845, Engels wrote \"The Condition of the Working Class in England,\" based on his observations in Manchester. The book was a damning critique of the industrial revolution's impact on workers and is considered a classic in sociopolitical literature.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Engels' Role in the Communist League</strong><br>\nEngels was actively involved in the Communist League, for which he and Marx co-authored \"The Communist Manifesto\" in 1848. The manifesto became one of the most influential political documents in the world, calling for the overthrow of capitalist societies.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Financial Support for Marx</strong><br>\nEngels supported Marx financially, allowing him to work on his economic theory and research that would later culminate in \"Das Kapital.\" Without Engels' support, Marx might not have been able to devote himself fully to his writing and theoretical work.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
