            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Engels' Later Work and Influence</strong><br>\nAfter Marx's death in 1883, Engels became the leading interpreter and advocate of Marxist theory. He published several works that further elaborated on their ideas and played a key role in promoting socialist movements in Europe.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Activism</strong><br>\nThroughout his life, Engels was deeply involved in political activism. He was a proponent of workers' rights and was influential in shaping socialist practices and the formulation of labor laws that sought to improve workers' conditions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Friedrich Engels</strong><br>\nFriedrich Engels died in 1895, but his contributions to social science and political theory continue to be relevant. His collaboration with Marx produced a body of work that has influenced political thought and action around the world for over a century.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
